{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgENUvwYUFxvTdtwS6Yam","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both bugs come from one cause: `hidden` is a DynamoDB reserved word, and `src/app.py` used it without an alias in two places. All 12 tests pass after the fix, and 7 of the new ones fail against the old code. I haven't deployed anything.\n\n- **Prod crash:** `hide()` wrote `SET hidden = ...`. DynamoDB rejects that with the \"reserved keyword\" error, which comes back as a 500, so the hide is never saved.\n- **Staging feed:** Dasha's write from the AWS CLI did save, because she aliased the name (`#h`). The problem is on the read side. `feed()` asks for `hidden` without an alias, and in that kind of read DynamoDB doesn't raise an error; it just leaves the field out. So the \"hidden\" filter never saw any value and removed nothing. It wasn't a cache or a wrong table. Fixing only the crash would have left hidden stories in the feed.\n- **A third one waiting to happen:** `archive()` checks `status` without an alias, and `status` is also reserved. It would have crashed the first time the admin console called it.\n\n**What I changed:**\n- **Aliases everywhere:** a small `_names()` helper in `src/app.py` now aliases every attribute name in every query, not just the reserved ones. Method names and signatures are unchanged.\n- **Feed paging:** `feed()` now reads every page of results. DynamoDB returns at most 1 MB per scan call, so a large table would have quietly lost stories. Nobody reported this; I fixed it while I was in that method.\n- **New tests** in `tests/test_feed.py`:\n  - Stories that are actually hidden, both through the library and by hand the way Dasha did it, are left out of the feed.\n  - Hiding or archiving a story that doesn't exist is rejected, and archiving a published story works.\n  - The feed reads all pages.\n  - A guard test that runs every library operation and fails if any query uses an attribute name without an alias. This is what keeps the bug from coming back.\n\n**Follow-up after you deploy:** the hides that crashed on prod (e.g. `why-i-left-mobx`) were never saved, so those stories need to be hidden again. `my-first-week` on staging is already marked hidden and will drop out of the feed once the new build is deployed.\n\nFrom the colleague's note, three parts applied: both failure modes (they match this case exactly), aliasing every name, and testing with the field actually set. I skipped the `similar` history, used a permanent test instead of a one-time grep, and didn't run the \"Verify\" step against real AWS because of the no-external-services rule. ANSWER.md has the details."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1914,"cache_read_input_tokens":34010,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1914},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a08a04c-3f1c-4797-98e7-f2dcbab8a0ac","uuid":"e5c8bd29-aa2c-4aaf-991a-8ea494019e97","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:39:28.130Z","request_id":"req_011CgENUvZy4acxWStgbQjhN"}
